# Video 1 — What Is Really Inside Text, and How Can We Prepare It?

---

## 00:00 The Text Looks Simple

[On screen: a text sample whose first line has two leading and two trailing spaces.]

```text
  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ

  second line
```

You may have pasted text into an AI tool to improve an email, reshape a paragraph, or suggest code. To us, the text can look simple: a few words, a blank line, perhaps an unusual symbol. Yet the software receives every mark that was entered, including marks our eyes may skip over.

This sample gives us a useful mystery. We can see spaces around the words, an empty line, a circled number, a wide letter, and a joined-looking symbol. There is also a mark before the second line that creates a gap but is difficult to see. Some marks even look interchangeable although software can tell them apart.

So here is our question: **How can we find out exactly what a piece of text contains and change it consistently before later AI work?**

By the end, you will be able to reveal marks that normal printing hides, explain why similar-looking characters may still differ, follow every change from the starting text to the result, and predict what happens when the starting text changes. We will not change anything by instinct. We will first find out what is actually there.

## 01:25 Reveal What Is Really There

First, we need the exact text before any changes. We call that starting text the **source text**, or simply the **source**.

Python stores text between quotation marks and calls the stored text a **string**. Here is our source string.

```python
source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "
```

The backslash combinations stand for marks that are hard to show on this line. What might normal printing hide? We may overlook the surrounding spaces, the mark before the second line, or the empty line.

We need a view that keeps those marks visible instead of turning them into spacing. Python calls this tool `repr`.

```python
print(repr(source))
```

```text
'  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  '
```

Now we have evidence. `\t` is the tab mark that created the larger gap. `\r\n` ends one line. Two together, `\r\n\r\n`, end the first line and then an empty line. The quotation marks show the string’s boundaries, making its surrounding spaces countable.

Spaces, tabs, and marks that end lines create gaps rather than visible letters. Together, Python calls them **whitespace**. Using `repr` to reveal the source is **inspection**. Inspection changes nothing; it only gives us a clearer view.

Hidden whitespace is now visible. But can two marks that are already visible also hide a difference?

## 03:05 Similar-Looking Marks Can Still Differ

Look at these two characters before reading any result.

```text
①    1
```

They can both communicate the idea of one in an ordinary sentence. Does that similar meaning force software to treat them as the same character? Pause for a prediction. If you think yes, how would software preserve the circle? If you think no, what fixed difference could we check without trusting our eyes?

Python can ask for the agreed number used to identify each character. It calls this tool `ord`. Run these two lines and compare the results.

```python
print(ord("①"))
print(ord("1"))
```

```text
9312
49
```

The numbers differ, so these are not the same character in this example. The result does not say that one is better or that either should change. It only reveals that the two characters have different agreed numbers. Now we can ask where that agreement comes from and what Python calls each number.

## 04:30 How Can Software Identify a Character?

Software needs a shared agreement so the same character can be identified consistently on different systems. The widely used agreement in our example is called **Unicode**.

Python’s `ord` tool asks Unicode for the number assigned to one character. That number has a technical name: a **code point**.

> In today’s examples, a code point is the fixed number Unicode uses to identify one character.

That explanation is deliberately narrow. Some visible symbols are built from several code points. Each symbol we inspect today uses one, so we can follow the numbers one at a time.

The earlier result now has a concrete meaning: `①` has code point `9312`, while `1` has code point `49`. The same check exposes two more differences that may be easy to miss when we read quickly.

```text
① -> 9312
1 -> 49

Ａ -> 65313
A -> 65

ﬀ -> [64256]
ff -> [102, 102]
```

The last pair is especially useful. `ﬀ` is one character in this example, while `ff` is two characters, so the brackets show one number on one side and two on the other. We have not prepared anything yet. We have only used code points to identify what the source contains. That difference in identity is exactly what makes a preparation choice necessary.

## 06:05 Which Differences Should We Preserve?

Suppose this text is meant to become one consistent source for our example. We can now name the choice in front of us. Should a circled one stay distinct from an ordinary one? Should a wide `Ａ` stay distinct from `A`? Should the joined-looking `ﬀ` remain one character, or become two letters?

There is no universal answer hidden in the code points. Code points identify characters; they do not tell us the purpose of the text. For code, poetry, mathematical notation, or historical text, a difference that is unimportant here may be meaningful and worth preserving.

For this example, we choose to treat each displayed pair as a consistent ordinary form. First notice the chosen behavior, without needing a label for it yet.

```text
① -> 1
Ａ -> A
ﬀ -> ff
```

We can make that choice because inspection exposed the source and code points exposed the identities. The next question is how to apply this one chosen consistency rule reliably instead of changing characters one by one.

## 07:30 Apply One Chosen Rule Consistently

We want the same selected changes every time: `①` should become `1`, `Ａ` should become `A`, and `ﬀ` should become `ff`. Applying a defined Unicode rule to produce chosen consistent forms is called **normalization**.

Unicode offers several normalization rules because different situations preserve different distinctions. The particular rule used in this lesson is named **NFKC**. For our three examples, NFKC produces exactly the changes we chose.

Notice that normalization can change more than appearance. In the first case, the string keeps the same length.

```text
① -> 1
length 1 -> 1
```

In the joined-looking case, one character becomes two.

```text
ﬀ -> ff
length 1 -> 2
```

That length change is why this is a rule we choose, not a visual polish we can assume is harmless. NFKC can remove distinctions that matter, so it is a chosen rule for this example—not a universal definition of clean text.

Normalization settles only the character forms we selected. Our source still has marks that end lines, an empty line, a tab, and surrounding spaces. We also want repeatable rules for those marks. Combining all these chosen changes into one repeatable process is what we will call **text preparation**.

## 09:05 Put the Chosen Steps Into One Recipe

Before looking at the code, let’s state the recipe. Apply NFKC to the source. Separate its lines. Remove spacing from both ends of each line. Drop empty lines. Rebuild what remains with one chosen mark between the lines.

Python gives three operations short names. Separating text where lines end is `splitlines`. Removing whitespace from both ends is `strip`. Rebuilding pieces with one chosen mark between them is `join`.

Python calls a reusable group of instructions a **function**. We will name ours `prepare_text`. The first line makes Python’s Unicode tools available. When the work is finished, the result must go back to the line that requested it; Python marks that final step with `return`.

```python
import unicodedata


def prepare_text(text):
    text = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
    lines = [line.strip() for line in text.splitlines()]
    non_empty_lines = [line for line in lines if line]
    return "\n".join(non_empty_lines)


source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "

print("Source text:", repr(source))
print("Prepared text:", repr(prepare_text(source)))
```

Follow the instructions in order. First, `unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)` changes `①`, `Ａ`, and `ﬀ`. The text is still one string, and all its whitespace remains.

Next, `splitlines` creates three lines: the first text line, an empty line, and the second text line. Then `strip` removes the spaces around the first line and the tab plus spaces around the second.

The square-bracket line keeps only lines containing text, so the empty line drops out. Finally, `"\n".join(non_empty_lines)` rebuilds the two remaining lines. Here, `\n` ends the first prepared line.

We can now trace the whole process without a hidden jump: source string, NFKC changes, three separated lines, whitespace removed from their ends, empty line removed, two lines rebuilt, prepared string.

The source used `\r\n` to end each line, but the result uses `\n`. Normalization did not cause this. The program separated the lines, then rebuilt them using the mark written before `join`.

## 11:15 Predict, Run, and Change the Starting Text

Save this exact program in a folder you control as `inspect_and_prepare_text.py`. Before you run it, make a complete prediction. Which characters will NFKC change? Which whitespace will `strip` remove? Will the empty line remain? Which mark will `join` place between the surviving lines? Use the recipe we traced, not a guess from how the source looks.

Then run it from that same working folder.

```bash
python inspect_and_prepare_text.py
```

```text
Source text: '  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  '
Prepared text: 'Lesson 1: A cat ff\nsecond line'
```

Compare the two lines shown by `repr`. NFKC changed `①` to `1`, `Ａ` to `A`, and `ﬀ` to `ff`. `splitlines` created three lines. `strip` removed spaces and the tab from their ends. The square-bracket line left out the empty line. `join` placed one `\n` between the lines that remained.

Now change only the source string. Keep the function exactly as it is.

```python
source = "  Chapter ②: Ｂig oﬀice  \n\n  final line  "
```

Predict the exact prepared line that `repr` will show. Which characters follow the same NFKC rule? Which preparation steps behave exactly as before? The changed words are not the point. The recipe should still explain the result.

```text
'Chapter 2: Big office\nfinal line'
```

Here, `②` becomes `2`, `Ｂ` becomes `B`, and `ﬀ` within `oﬀice` becomes `ff`. The outer whitespace disappears, the empty line disappears, and one newline joins the two text lines. Because only the starting text changed, this second result tests whether your explanation transfers rather than whether you remembered the first output.

Do not treat the changed output as a new rule to memorize. It is evidence that the same function can be traced again. If your prediction differed, return to the first operation that would produce that difference. That habit is more useful than guessing what a preparation function ought to do.

## 13:30 Carry the Explanation Forward

We can now answer the central question without treating the changes as a mystery. The unchanged starting text is the source. Inspection reveals what that source actually contains. A code point identifies one character in today’s examples. Normalization applies a chosen Unicode rule. Text preparation combines normalization with the other chosen changes to lines and whitespace.

The important distinction is simple: identification tells us what is present, while preparation applies decisions about what to change for a stated purpose. We inspected before changing anything, chose NFKC with its limit in view, and traced every step from the source string to the prepared result.

That order protects us from a common mistake: seeing a difference and assuming it should disappear. A difference can be real, while the decision to preserve or change it still depends on the purpose we stated. Inspection gives us facts. Preparation applies our chosen rules to those facts.

When you meet a new text sample, start the same way. Look for the actual marks, identify the differences you need to reason about, choose rules that fit the purpose, and test the result with changed starting text. Prepared text is now a dependable building block for the next lesson.
